=== task: mcq medium
title: Why wait for 0?
prompt: In Kahn's algorithm, why does a job join the queue only when its count reaches 0, rather than as soon as its count goes down?
* Because only a count of 0 means all its prerequisites are finished; joining earlier could put it before a prerequisite :: Right. A job with a count of 1 still waits for something.
- To keep the queue short, though joining earlier would give the same order :: Joining earlier gives wrong orders: a job could be taken before one of its prerequisites.
- Because counts can only change once :: A job's count drops once for every prerequisite, so it can change several times.
- Because a job with a count of 1 is part of a cycle :: A count of 1 only means one prerequisite is unfinished, which is normal.
hint: What does a count of 1 mean?
hint: What would go wrong if cook joined the queue when only boil was finished?
right: The count is the number of unfinished prerequisites. Only 0 means "free", so joining at 0 is what keeps every job after all its prerequisites.
wrong: A job whose count has dropped but isn't 0 still has unfinished prerequisites. Queuing it then would let it be done too early.

